        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01TqwMjrHNYtgUApfbFE2Fzt",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022pages/concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point Architecture\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime-path resolution converge on a single composition point, enabling child processes to receive both secrets and configuration without ambient environment mutation.\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - tech/dotnet\\n  - architectural-pattern/Composition\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly describe the same architectural pattern\u2014a single startup resolver that prepares everything (secrets, paths, configuration) before spawning child processes, then injects resolved values into those processes without mutating the parent\u0027s environment.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2027-01-14\\n---\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] describe the same underlying architectural principle: **a single composition point at startup that resolves and prepares all runtime context\u2014both secrets and filesystem configuration\u2014before child processes begin, then injects that context into each child process without mutating the parent\u0027s ambient environment.**\\n\\nThe two decisions are not separate; they are expressions of the same pattern applied to different concerns:\\n\\n- **Credential scoping** solves a security concern: the Hub\u0027s process environment never holds the API key. A secrets loader reads it from \u0060.env\u0060, then injects it into the child process\u0027s environment at spawn time.\\n- **Runtime-path resolution** solves a composability concern: all filesystem locations are resolved in exactly one place at startup (from config, environment, defaults, in precedence order), validated, and reported. Child processes (and all downstream code) consume this pre-resolved path set rather than rediscovering locations.\\n\\nBoth mechanisms operate at the same architectural layer: the composition point. Both prevent scattered runtime rediscovery. Both rely on a single source of truth to construct the context each agent needs.\\n\\n## Why This Matters\\n\\nThis pattern decouples two critical properties:\\n\\n1. **Parent process hygiene**: The Hub itself remains free of both secrets and environment-dependent path assumptions. It holds neither the credential nor the burden of resolving where every file lives.\\n2. **Child process determinism**: Each spawned agent (Ingest, Query, or any future agent) receives a complete, pre-validated, immutable context\u2014both the secrets it needs and the filesystem locations it depends on\u2014injected at startup. The agent can trust that its environment is correct and complete.\\n\\nAdding a new agent or new runtime location does not require scattering logic across the codebase. It means extending the single composition resolver to handle the new concern, then adjusting the injection point. The pattern scales.\\n\\n## Related Concepts\\n\\n- [[credential-scoping]] \u2014 the security expression of this pattern\\n- [[runtime-paths]] \u2014 the filesystem-configuration expression of this pattern\\n\u0022}"
        }
