      "assistant_text": "Given the constraint, let me answer your question directly based on what I\u0027ve read, and note that the synthesis page has been created:\n\n---\n\n## How credential-scoping and runtime-paths relate\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are **two expressions of the same underlying architectural principle**: a **single composition point at startup** that resolves and prepares all runtime context\u2014both secrets and filesystem configuration\u2014before child processes begin, then injects that context into each process without mutating the parent\u0027s environment.\n\nFrom [[credential-scoping]]: the Hub\u0027s process environment never holds the API key. A secrets loader reads it from \u0060.env\u0060 at startup and injects it into the spawned Ingest (and Query) agent\u0027s environment at spawn time, keeping the Hub\u0027s own environment clean.\n\nFrom [[runtime-paths]]: every filesystem location the Hub touches\u2014the wiki root, data directories, the database, the secrets file, agent instruction directories\u2014is resolved in exactly one place at startup. A single path resolver reads configuration, validates it, and reports effective paths. No part of the codebase rediscovers locations on its own; everything downstream consumes the pre-resolved path set.\n\nBoth mechanisms operate at the same architectural layer. Both prevent scattered runtime rediscovery. Both rely on a single source of truth to construct the context each agent needs. **The pattern scales**: adding a new agent or new runtime location means extending that one composition resolver, not scattering logic across the codebase.\n\nThe connection is this: **parent process hygiene** (the Hub holds neither secrets nor environment-dependent assumptions) and **child process determinism** (each agent receives a complete, pre-validated, immutable context) are joint consequences of the same design decision.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]].",
